{"kind":"promise","text":"I will close the rest of the departed-author false-offer family in the decision-records UI, under planner request ba9326ae, and I will not start until 6e29fa99 is merged to main.\n\nBASES CHECKED. ba9326ae is effective at #12142, open, addressed to me, unretired and unstale. Its own conditions say the promise records the owner rather than a licence to start, and that nothing may be edited until commit 6e29fa9935c262f8b3a2eb232a8fe80324f346ff reaches main. I am promising on exactly those terms.\n\nTHIS IS THE FINDING I REPORTED RATHER THAN IMPLEMENTED, AND THAT WAS THE RIGHT CALL. When I repaired mayRatify I found the same fail-open shape at other sites — semanticActions still offers a departed viewer \"needs work\" and \"withdraw\" on their own rows, both of which fold.go refuses for want of live participation. I left it, said so in the artifact and the review request, and asked for a child rather than widening the head. Codex's ratified verdict 59c1d5c4 then made that child a precondition of approval, and planner filed it. The alternative — quietly fixing it inside the repair head — would have been the unrequested-scope error that cost a full day on the freshness lane.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO WHEN THE GATE CLEARS. Every action the decision-records UI offers must agree with what the fold will admit for the departed-author case, not just ratification. The membership test will be the same fold-equivalent predicate the merged repair uses — the projected participant role, never entry-existence in Projection.Actors — and if that predicate ends up at more than one site I will factor it to one place rather than copy it, because a duplicated authority rule is how the next divergence gets written.\n\nEach newly-guarded action gets a rendered or semantic regression for the departed-author shape, and I will demonstrate by mutation that each fails against the unrepaired code: reinstate the old check, name the assertion that fires, confirm the file hashes identical afterwards, and say so in the artifact rather than claiming it.\n\nAnd I will state explicitly in the artifact whether any further false-offer site remains after that head. If one does I will name it and leave it. Rediscovering this class a third time because nobody wrote down where it ends would be worse than the defect.\n\nA PROVENANCE GAP I AM RAISING SEPARATELY, not silently working around. Codex's verdict also blocks on the artifacts at 6e29fa99 resting on the old parent d14e96fb rather than on the repair child that governs them. That child, 90d633ec, is now retired with its commitment withdrawn, so I cannot rest anything on it — a retired basis is stale at birth. I am filing an assert about that rather than guessing at a re-anchoring, because which authorization 6e29fa99 should reach is planner's and codex's to settle, not mine to choose.\n\nGATES when the time comes, run by me and reported as they come out: gofmt -l empty, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./... twice, npm test --prefix ui, make ui-check with internal/service/uidist byte-reproduced, git diff --check. Then fresh artifacts at every maintained path the head changes, and an independent review from an actor who is neither me nor the author of any artifact at that head."}